      "id": 1,
      "text": "Millinocket’s name comes from the Abenaki word meaning “land of many islands.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://millinocket.org/visitors/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Betsy and Thomas Fowler settled the area in 1829, clearing a farm near Shad Pond.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millinocket%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Bangor and Aroostook Railroad reached Houlton in 1894, opening Millinocket to rail development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millinocket%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1846, Henry David Thoreau hired the Fowler family as guides for his Mount Katahdin trip.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millinocket%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Millinocket’s economy relied on forest products and a paper mill that closed in 2008.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millinocket%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
